
<p>Jonathan Stempel, reporting for Reuters:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>General Motors will pay $220 million to settle ​allegations that
it illegally sold drivers’ private behavior and location data to
brokers, who then sold the information to insurers that used it to
set and sometimes increase rates.</p>

<p>The settlement, announced on Thursday, resolves claims by the
attorneys general of 35 US states and the District ​of Columbia
over the automaker’s Smart Driver program, which enrolled millions
of drivers ​between 2016 and its discontinuation in 2024. Other
regulators have also reviewed GM’s handling of driver data.</p>

<p>GM was accused of pitching Smart Driver to help ​people improve
their personal driving safety, only to violate drivers’ privacy by
reneging on its ​promise not to sell their data without
permission. Many people who signed up were also enrolled in GM’s
OnStar safety, navigation and diagnostic service.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Reuters’s milquetoast headline was “GM to Pay $220 Million to Settle Allegations It Violated Drivers’ Privacy”. They’re not mere allegations. They did it. They just betrayed their customers’ privacy to make a few quick bucks.</p>

<p>I’ve driven a few GM rentals in recent years, and I’d almost certainly never buy one of their cars just because they’re poorly-designed pieces of crap. But even if they made a decent car I wouldn’t buy it. They’re still seemingly <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/09/21/gm-crack-smoking">digging in on the CarPlay thing</a>, and they ratted out their own customers to insurance companies.</p>

